{"parentUuid":"89d34a0d-dcad-4097-a5ce-c9aad3f6adc9","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a7322ed66982214c7","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01FM5cRZDhDuEza1tjDuH4G5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012EEnLPmUzPdmrGV7jjEUWP","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"RINS relaxation induced neighborhood search MIP heuristic vs LNS"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":944,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4784,"cache_read_input_tokens":26988,"output_tokens":449,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4784},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":944,"output_tokens":449,"cache_read_input_tokens":26988,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4784,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4784,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaotpxRSmUssxxKD3hssn","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"9879db04-f978-4061-afd8-ac05390a4c7b","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:10:09.184Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"5035a682-4609-4fad-a8ab-bc11e0d3990a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"263c4537-7eaf-4141-8243-2bd92971b2f2","agentId":"a7322ed66982214c7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015jwrtjx4hWW3fA1ZtdYQxq","type":"tool_result","content":"Based on the provided content, I cannot give you the specific technical details you're requesting about Hexaly's internal local search implementation.\n\nThe article discusses local search as a **general optimization technique** but does not detail Hexaly's proprietary algorithms. It mentions that local search can use \"**multi-start strategies**\" and references external metaheuristics like \"**Variable Neighborhood Search (VNS)**\" and \"**Large Neighborhood Search (LNS)**\" as extensions, but does not specify which Hexaly actually employs.\n\nThe content states: \"In routing problems, a move might swap two visits in a route. In scheduling problems, it might change the assignment of a task to a different machine or time slot.\" However, this describes general move types, not Hexaly-specific implementations.\n\nTo find Hexaly's actual algorithmic components, move definitions, and adaptive memory strategies, you would need to consult their technical documentation, API references, or contact them directly through the resources listed on their site."}]},"uuid":"fa252014-5c43-4b57-b4ad-8cc3ea9cde76","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:10:10.955Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5035a682-4609-4fad-a8ab-bc11e0d3990a","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"40f2f325-7d0d-4808-88b7-c3469ada30c5","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"263c4537-7eaf-4141-8243-2bd92971b2f2","agentId":"a7322ed66982214c7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RFiCeAG8sn7eMoi9wsx1zi","type":"tool_result","content":"# ALNS Library Capabilities\n\n## Supported Components\n\nThe library provides **acceptance criteria, operator selection schemes, and stopping criteria**, though the documentation excerpt doesn't detail specific implementations of each.\n\n## Example Problems\n\nThe library includes five documented examples:\n\n1. **Travelling Salesman Problem (TSP)** - 131 cities\n2. **Capacitated Vehicle Routing Problem (CVRP)** - 241 customers\n3. **Cutting-Stock Problem (CSP)** - 180 beams\n4. **Resource-Constrained Project Scheduling (RCPSP)** - 90 jobs\n5. **Permutation Flow Shop Problem (PFSP)** - 50 jobs, 20 machines\n\n## Suitability for 2D Placement Problems\n\n**Not indicated in the provided documentation.** The library focuses on combinatorial optimization problems like routing, scheduling, and packing. While placement problems are combinatorial in nature, the documentation makes no explicit mention of 2D placement support or geometry-based functionality. The framework appears flexible enough to accommodate such problems if you implement appropriate destroy/repair operators, but this isn't demonstrated in the examples provided."}]},"uuid":"34b608d4-aef8-4c79-9fe5-bbabb6750e97","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:10:12.286Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"40f2f325-7d0d-4808-88b7-c3469ada30c5","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"89d34a0d-dcad-4097-a5ce-c9aad3f6adc9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"263c4537-7eaf-4141-8243-2bd92971b2f2","agentId":"a7322ed66982214c7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R37LzgZcjmDJMCiWcbJEgx","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"large neighborhood search\" CP-SAT hint warm start incumbent OR-Tools combine\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Large Neighborhood Search - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/09_lns.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"How does it work? - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/07_under_the_hood.html\"},{\"title\":\"7.7. Large neighborhood search (LNS): the Job-Shop Problem — or-tools User's Manual\",\"url\":\"https://acrogenesis.com/or-tools/documentation/user_manual/manual/metaheuristics/jobshop_lns.html\"},{\"title\":\"📈 #76 Optimizing with Google OR-Tools: strategies and insights from three years of real-world experience\",\"url\":\"https://www.feasible.club/p/76-optimizing-with-google-or-tools\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT for scheduling Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://schedulingseminar.com/presentations/SchedulingSeminar_LaurentPerron.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT, UNKNOWN status with complete solution hint · Issue #3750 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3750\"}]\n\nI found comprehensive information about your search query on large neighborhood search, CP-SAT, warm start, and incumbent solutions in OR-Tools. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Large Neighborhood Search in CP-SAT\n\nCP-SAT implements large neighborhood search (i.e., RINS), which is a core technique used by the solver.\n\n## How LNS Works\n\nLNS formulates a \"mini-problem\" that modifies parts of the current solution, typically involving randomly selecting some variables, resetting them, and using CP-SAT to find the optimal new values within the context of the remaining solution.\n\n## Strategy Scheduling\n\nCP-SAT schedules its LNS strategies using a simple round-robin method, and whenever a worker thread finishes its work, it will execute an iteration with the next strategy in the list, leading to a fair distribution of calls.\n\n## Hints and Warm Starting\n\nIf you have a good intuition about how the solution might look, you can inform CP-SAT to incorporate this knowledge into its search through hints, which some workers will try to follow, and if the hints represent a feasible solution, the solver can use them to prune the search space of all branches that have worse bounds than the hints.\n\n## Implementation Details\n\nWhen performing LNS, a copy of the model is made with a solution selected from the pool, a subset of variables is removed, the remaining variables are fixed to their current values, the simplified model is presolved, and then solved using a complete strategy with a short time limit on a single thread, with any new solution added to the pool.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"310b4f46-e233-4139-87ae-678a29209f0f","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:10:16.853Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"89d34a0d-dcad-4097-a5ce-c9aad3f6adc9","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"9879db04-f978-4061-afd8-ac05390a4c7b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"263c4537-7eaf-4141-8243-2bd92971b2f2","agentId":"a7322ed66982214c7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012EEnLPmUzPdmrGV7jjEUWP","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"RINS relaxation induced neighborhood search MIP heuristic vs LNS\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Exploring relaxation induced neighborhoods to improve MIP solutions | Mathematical Programming | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10107-004-0518-7\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) RENS-relaxation enforced neighborhood search\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/228715008_RENS-relaxation_enforced_neighborhood_search\"},{\"title\":\"A pre-processing aware RINS based MIP heuristic\",\"url\":\"http://www.decom.ufop.br/haroldo/papers/Gomes2013.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] Exploring relaxation induced neighborhoods to improve MIP solutions | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Exploring-relaxation-induced-neighborhoods-to-MIP-Danna-Rothberg/74e86f325ddfb08f2f79b488ebfac2a43fd5e529\"},{\"title\":\"Neural Large Neighborhood Search Ravichandra Addanki MIT addanki@mit.edu\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/pdf?id=xEQhKANoVW\"},{\"title\":\"Exploring relaxation induced neighborhoods to improve MILP solutions | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/200035270_Exploring_relaxation_induced_neighborhoods_to_improve_MILP_solutions\"},{\"title\":\"Adaptive large neighborhood search for mixed integer programming | Mathematical Programming Computation | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12532-021-00209-7\"},{\"title\":\"Exploring relaxation induced neighborhoods to improve MIP solutions | Mathematical Programming: Series A and B\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1007/s10107-004-0518-7\"},{\"title\":\"Large Neighborhood Search (LNS) for Mixed-Integer Linear Programming (MILP) | Jongmin Mun\",\"url\":\"https://jong-min.org/blog/2026/large-neighborhood-search-milp/\"},{\"title\":\"Searching Large Neighborhoods for Integer Linear Programs ...\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v202/huang23g/huang23g.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's a comprehensive overview of RINS (Relaxation Induced Neighborhood Search) as a MIP heuristic in relation to Large Neighborhood Search (LNS):\n\n## RINS as a Large Neighborhood Search Heuristic\n\nRINS constructs a promising neighborhood using information contained in the continuous relaxation of the MIP model. RINS is a well-known large neighborhood search meta-heuristic for improving a given feasible MIP solution.\n\n## How RINS Works\n\nRINS works by comparing the current best incumbent integer solution against the optimal solution of the continuous LP relaxation at the current node of the branch-and-bound tree. If variables agree on both solutions, that variable is temporarily fixed to that value. If they disagree, the variable is left \"free.\" A sub-MIP is formulated on the free variables and handed to the solver with a strict node or time limit.\n\nRINS leverages the intuition that variables showing consensus between the continuous relaxation and the integer incumbent are highly likely to maintain those values in an optimal or near-optimal solution.\n\n## Relationship to LNS\n\nLNS heuristics use the incumbent solution as starting points and define the neighborhood to be a sub-MIP of the original MIP which is constructed by fixing variables and adding constraints. RINS is a specialization within the Large Neighborhood Search (LNS) paradigm.\n\n## Performance\n\nComputational experiments on very difficult MIP models show that RINS outperforms default CPLEX MIP and a previously described approach for exploring MIP neighborhoods (local branching). RINS is a highly effective generic heuristic designed by Danna, Rothberg, and Le Pape (2005) and heavily utilized by IBM CPLEX and Gurobi.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"cfb1e65d-3920-41af-b99d-da081cbf21b9","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:10:17.638Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9879db04-f978-4061-afd8-ac05390a4c7b","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
